      "problem": "Given that $u=1+{x}^{2}$, which integral is equivalent to $\\int {x}^{5}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x?$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $u=1+{x}^{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,x\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that \n${x}^{2}=u-1.$\n\nNow we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{5}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot x\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (u-1)^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (u-1)^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$",
